<h2 id="usage">Usage</h2>

<div class="nsw-grid nsw-m-top-sm">
  <div class="nsw-col nsw-col-sm-6 nsw-docs__guideline">
    <div class="nsw-docs__guideline--do"><span class="material-icons nsw-material-icons" focusable="false" aria-hidden="true">check_circle</span><span>Use Easy Read when</span></div>
    <ul>
      <li>research shows the audience needs or prefers Easy Read</li>
      <li>explaining rights, choices and responsibilities</li>
      <li>sharing health, safety or emergency information</li>
      <li>helping people access services or support</li>
      <li>supporting an important task, form or application</li>
      <li>communicating changes that affect a person's daily life</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="nsw-col nsw-col-sm-6 nsw-docs__guideline">
    <div class="nsw-docs__guideline--dont"><span class="material-icons nsw-material-icons" focusable="false" aria-hidden="true">cancel</span><span>Avoid Easy Read when</span></div>
    <ul>
      <li>audience research does not support using Easy Read</li>
      <li>simplifying would remove detail needed for a safe or informed decision</li>
      <li>users need other formats such as specialist translations, Auslan, audio, accessible documents or personalised support</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</div>

<h2 id="create-easy-read">Suggested process</h2>
<p>The following steps are suggestions that teams can adapt to their audience, content and context. They are not NSW requirements.</p>
<div class="nsw-steps nsw-steps--supplementary nsw-steps--small">
  <div class="nsw-steps__item">
    <div class="nsw-steps__content">
      <h3 class="nsw-h4">Understand the audience and task</h3>
      <p>Use research to identify who needs the information, what they need to know or do, how they access it and what support or alternative formats they use.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="nsw-steps__item">
    <div class="nsw-steps__content">
      <h3 class="nsw-h4">Prepare a source brief</h3>
      <p>Record the source material, content owners, publication dates and facts that are important to keep accurate.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="nsw-steps__item">
    <div class="nsw-steps__content">
      <h3 class="nsw-h4">Assess risk</h3>
      <p>Look for legal, clinical, financial, eligibility, deadline, emergency and physical-safety information. Involve a subject matter expert for higher-risk content.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="nsw-steps__item">
    <div class="nsw-steps__content">
      <h3 class="nsw-h4">Plan the words and images</h3>
      <p>Connect each main concept or action to its source information. Describe what each image helps the audience understand.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="nsw-steps__item">
    <div class="nsw-steps__content">
      <h3 class="nsw-h4">Draft and review</h3>
      <p>Compare meaning with the source before refining readability or visual style to reveal gaps and conflicts.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="nsw-steps__item">
    <div class="nsw-steps__content">
      <h3 class="nsw-h4">Test and revise</h3>
      <p>Involve people from the intended audience during discovery, in early words-and-images testing and when reviewing the complete experience.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="nsw-steps__item">
    <div class="nsw-steps__content">
      <h3 class="nsw-h4">Publish and maintain</h3>
      <p>Record reviews and testing, use accessible HTML as the primary format, check other formats, and nominate an owner and review date.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<h3>Prepare an authoring brief</h3>
<p>Include:</p>
<ul>
  <li>the intended audience, their task and evidence that Easy Read is suitable</li>
  <li>the source material, its owner, version and date</li>
  <li>facts, names, definitions, numbers and exact terms that are important to preserve</li>
  <li>conditions, exceptions, deadlines, costs, consequences and ways to get help</li>
  <li>possible reviewers, an audience-testing approach and alternative formats</li>
  <li>unknown, disputed or missing information for a person to clarify</li>
</ul>
<p>For higher-risk content, give each source fact an identifier and record which facts appear in each Easy Read block. This helps reviewers check whether simplification has changed or removed important meaning.</p>

<div class="nsw-callout nsw-m-top-md">
  <div class="nsw-callout__content">
    <p><strong>Audience testing is strongly encouraged.</strong> Describe content as a plain-language or Easy Read draft until people from the intended audience have reviewed the words, images and complete task. Readability tools, accessibility checkers and AI tools can support the process but are not a substitute for audience feedback.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2 id="content-design">1. Content design</h2>
<p>Plan the words and pictures together. Give each content block one primary concept or action to make the page easier to understand and use.</p>

<h3>Writing content</h3>

<h4>Write for your audience</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Write for the identified audience and the task they need to complete.</li>
  <li>Use respectful, adult language that preserves the person's dignity, agency and right to make decisions.</li>
  <li>Avoid assuming a support person will read, decide or act for the person.</li>
  <li>Keep the content concise without removing information needed for a safe or informed decision.</li>
</ul>

<h4>Preserve meaning and accuracy</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Compare factual statements with the source brief.</li>
  <li>Keep important conditions, exceptions, deadlines, costs, consequences and support options.</li>
  <li>Retain exact legal, clinical, service and eligibility terms when people will encounter them elsewhere, then explain them clearly.</li>
  <li>Ask the source owner about ambiguity or missing information rather than guessing.</li>
  <li>Seek review from the content owner or a subject matter expert when the content is higher risk.</li>
</ul>

<h4>Organise the information</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Use headings to break up content into sections.</li>
  <li>Begin new ideas on a new line or in a new paragraph.</li>
</ul>
<p>Keep detail pages focused. If a page becomes too long or starts covering multiple topics, split it into smaller topic or detail pages and connect them through the landing and category structure.</p>

<h4>Make instructions complete</h4>
<p>Check that people can find the details they need to act. Include:</p>
<ul>
  <li>who needs to act and what they need to do</li>
  <li>where to go or which verified link, phone number or service to use</li>
  <li>what they need before they start</li>
  <li>costs, deadlines, waiting times, conditions and exceptions</li>
  <li>what happens next and what to do if the task does not work</li>
  <li>where to get accessible or person-to-person support</li>
</ul>

<h4>Write clear sentences</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Keep sentences under 25 words where possible.</li>
  <li>Communicate one idea per sentence.</li>
  <li>Use active voice.</li>
  <li>Avoid questions.</li>
</ul>

<h4>Choose familiar words</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Use common words.</li>
  <li>Explain difficult words immediately.</li>
  <li>Avoid jargon, idioms and unexplained acronyms.</li>
</ul>

<h4>Use simple punctuation and formatting</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Use simple punctuation and avoid colons and semicolons.</li>
  <li>Avoid italics or underlining, except for links.</li>
  <li>Avoid text in all capitals.</li>
</ul>

<h4>Present numbers, dates and links clearly</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Use numerals for numbers unless audience testing supports another form.</li>
  <li>Write dates in full and include the day of the week when it helps people plan.</li>
  <li>Use the 12-hour clock with <strong>am</strong> or <strong>pm</strong>.</li>
  <li>Explain percentages with a concrete expression where useful, such as <strong>1 in 4 people</strong>.</li>
  <li>Use clear, descriptive link labels that make sense out of context and lead to a verified destination.</li>
</ul>

<h4>Check and test the content</h4>
<ul>
  <li>A Flesch-Kincaid Grade Level of 4 to 6 can be a useful diagnostic target rather than a pass or fail test. A score does not assess accuracy, dignity, image meaning or whether a person can complete the task.</li>
  <li>Test the content with people who use Easy Read.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Difficult words and definitions</h3>
<p>Use a common word when it keeps the meaning accurate. When a difficult or specialist word is necessary, make it bold the first time and explain it immediately in the next sentence. Use the same term consistently after defining it.</p>
<p>Keep a terms list that records why each difficult term is kept, its plain explanation, where it first appears and whether the intended audience understood it.</p>
<p>Explain essential instructions on the page rather than making people follow a link or open a separate glossary.</p>

<h3>Images and illustrations</h3>
<p>Images can form part of the meaning in Easy Read rather than acting as decoration. Aim to make the connection between an image and the words beside it quick and clear.</p>
<p>This guidance applies when selecting, commissioning or creating images. If you use AI to create images, also follow the guidance for <a href="#ai-generated-images">creating images with AI</a>.</p>

<h4>Image specifications</h4>
<table aria-label="Specifications for Easy Read images">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th scope="col">Specification</th>
      <th scope="col">Guidance</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <th scope="row">Content type</th>
      <td>Choose a photograph, illustration or consistent photo-illustration hybrid based on the image's purpose and what the audience understands. An illustration can simplify unnecessary detail. A photograph can help people recognise a particular person, place, object or action. Test the choice with people who use Easy Read.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <th scope="row">Subject</th>
      <td>Show one clear concept, object or action that directly supports the adjacent words. Make the main subject immediately recognisable.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <th scope="row">Composition</th>
      <td>Use a single focal point, a close or medium view and generous clear space. Show a complete action and include only objects needed to understand it.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <th scope="row">Dimensions</th>
      <td>Choose the display ratio before creating or selecting an image. The landscape content block displays images at <strong>3:2</strong>. For raster images, supply enough resolution for the largest display size; <strong>1200 × 800 pixels</strong> is a useful source size. Crop deliberately and keep meaningful content away from the outer edges.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <th scope="row">File format</th>
      <td>Use an optimised PNG when an illustration or hybrid needs transparency. Use an optimised JPEG or WebP for photographs that fill the frame. Only use SVG for trusted, sanitised vector artwork.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <th scope="row">Background</th>
      <td>For illustrations and hybrids, use a transparent background with no border, frame, shadow or decorative scenery. For photographs, use a plain, uncluttered setting with strong separation between the subject and background.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <th scope="row">Style and colour</th>
      <td>Use natural proportions, clear shapes and realistic, easily recognised colours. Keep perspective, line weight, lighting and level of detail consistent throughout a page or journey.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <th scope="row">People</th>
      <td>Represent the diversity of NSW respectfully. Use natural expressions and body language, avoid stereotypes and only show identifying details when they help explain the concept.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <th scope="row">Text and branding</th>
      <td>Include text in an image only if it helps identify or explain what the image shows, such as a necessary sign or label. Keep it short and legible. Provide essential information as accessible HTML next to the image, and include meaningful image text in the alternative text when it is not available elsewhere.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <th scope="row">Quality checks</th>
      <td>Check anatomy, objects, clothing, signs, spatial relationships and the sequence of actions. Confirm the image is accurate, culturally safe, licensed and understood by people who use Easy Read.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h4>Prefer and avoid</h4>
<div class="nsw-grid nsw-m-top-sm">
  <div class="nsw-col nsw-col-sm-6 nsw-docs__guideline">
    <div class="nsw-docs__guideline--do"><span class="material-icons nsw-material-icons" focusable="false" aria-hidden="true">check_circle</span><span>Prefer</span></div>
    <ul>
      <li>one obvious subject or action</li>
      <li>a simple composition with a clear focal point</li>
      <li>recognisable objects shown from a familiar angle</li>
      <li>natural expressions, poses and proportions</li>
      <li>inclusive, respectful and culturally safe representation</li>
      <li>consistent image type and visual style across a journey</li>
      <li>transparent PNG illustrations with clean edges</li>
      <li>images that still make sense at a smaller size</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="nsw-col nsw-col-sm-6 nsw-docs__guideline">
    <div class="nsw-docs__guideline--dont"><span class="material-icons nsw-material-icons" focusable="false" aria-hidden="true">cancel</span><span>Avoid</span></div>
    <ul>
      <li>multiple concepts, actions or competing focal points</li>
      <li>busy backgrounds, decorative props or unnecessary detail</li>
      <li>abstract metaphors, visual jokes or symbolism that relies on inference</li>
      <li>dramatic camera angles, extreme crops or distorted perspective</li>
      <li>mixing photography, illustration and hybrid styles without a reason</li>
      <li>stereotypes, exaggerated emotion or distressing imagery</li>
      <li>text, labels or logos that do not help identify or explain the image</li>
      <li>watermarks, borders or drop shadows</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</div>


<h4>Choosing and publishing images</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Choose the image type based on its purpose and what the audience understands. Test whether an illustration, photograph or hybrid communicates the concept best.</li>
  <li>Show one concept per image and keep the visual treatment consistent across the page.</li>
  <li>Limit unnecessary background detail. Include text in an image only if it helps identify or explain what the image shows.</li>
  <li>Use respectful representation and review imagery for cultural safety.</li>
  <li>Give informative images meaningful alternative text that describes the information they add.</li>
  <li>Only use the explicit <code>decorative</code> option when an image adds no information.</li>
  <li>Write alternative text after inspecting the final image, not from its filename, prompt or intended design.</li>
  <li>If an image repeats the adjacent words and adds no other information, use empty alternative text. If it adds a person, object, action, sequence, destination or warning needed to understand the content, describe that meaning concisely.</li>
  <li>For linked images, describe the link purpose through the link text or accessible name rather than repeating a literal description of the image.</li>
  <li>Test whether the intended audience understands each image and its relationship to the text.</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="using-ai">Using AI to help create Easy Read</h3>
<p>AI can help analyse source material, identify difficult language, propose a block plan, draft alternatives and create image concepts. Human judgement and audience feedback can help assess what the audience needs, factual accuracy, accessibility and whether the content works in practice.</p>
<p>Label AI output <strong>draft – not validated as Easy Read</strong> while it is being reviewed and tested.</p>

<h4>Information to give AI</h4>
<p>Provide the audience and task, source text, important facts, difficult terms, risk level, actions and support options. Ask the AI to:</p>
<ul>
  <li>base factual claims on the supplied sources</li>
  <li>map each proposed block to the source information it communicates</li>
  <li>keep names, dates, amounts, conditions, exceptions and consequences</li>
  <li>list assumptions, source conflicts and missing information for review</li>
  <li>focus each block on one main concept or action</li>
  <li>flag difficult terms, long sentences and blocks that may contain more than one concept</li>
  <li>propose an image purpose and concept for each block</li>
</ul>

<h4 id="ai-generated-images">Creating images with AI</h4>
<p>The image guidance above applies to AI-generated images as well as selected or commissioned images. When creating an image with AI:</p>
<ul>
  <li>use the image purpose and specifications as a brief</li>
  <li>treat the output as a draft that needs human review and testing with people who use Easy Read</li>
  <li>add any required words during design or production rather than relying on AI to render them accurately</li>
  <li>check for AI artefacts such as malformed anatomy, missing or duplicated objects, unreadable or incorrect text and impossible spatial relationships</li>
</ul>

<h4>Risks to watch for</h4>
<p>When reviewing AI output, check whether it has:</p>
<ul>
  <li>invented or guessed facts, definitions, links, phone numbers, deadlines, costs or eligibility rules</li>
  <li>removed a condition or exception to make content shorter</li>
  <li>presented a draft as compliant, approved, accessible, user tested or ready to publish</li>
  <li>recorded a human approval or testing result that a person did not provide</li>
  <li>written alternative text without access to the final image</li>
  <li>become the only source of instructions for legal, clinical, emergency, financial, eligibility or physical-safety decisions</li>
</ul>

<h4>Make the output easier to review</h4>
<p>Ask the AI to return the draft with supporting information such as:</p>
<ul>
  <li>a draft status</li>
  <li>the audience, task, sources and risk level it was given</li>
  <li>source references for each content block</li>
  <li>unresolved questions, assumptions and possible omissions</li>
  <li>a terms list and proposed explanations</li>
  <li>image purposes and concepts for each block</li>
  <li>link destinations and potentially missing task details</li>
  <li>space to record human review and audience feedback</li>
</ul>
<p>Comparing the output with the brief, checking factual completeness with a source owner and gathering audience feedback can all help improve a draft.</p>

<h4>AI-generated content and transparency</h4>
<p>Decide whether a disclosure would help people understand where artificial intelligence (AI) was used to create or materially change published content. Follow relevant organisational policies.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Use a clear, plain-language disclosure that identifies the content created or changed using AI.</li>
  <li>Place the disclosure next to the relevant content or in a consistent note on the same page.</li>
  <li>Describe the actual use of AI rather than saying a page <em>may</em> contain AI-generated content.</li>
  <li>Use the final Easy Read callout or another location that suits the page.</li>
  <li>Review generated content for bias, stereotypes, harmful representation and factual or visual errors.</li>
  <li>Seek review from an appropriate subject matter expert for safety-critical content.</li>
  <li>Check licensing and organisational policies before using generated content.</li>
</ul>


<h2 id="page-structure">2. Page structure</h2>
<p>Keep Easy Read structures shallow and predictable. For small sets of content, link directly to detail pages. For larger collections, use the information architecture below as a starting point.</p>

<h3 id="information-architecture">Information architecture for larger collections</h3>
<figure class="nsw-easy-read-ia">
  <ol class="nsw-easy-read-ia__list">
    <li>
      <div class="nsw-easy-read-ia__node nsw-easy-read-ia__node--landing">
        <strong>Landing page</strong>
        <span>Shows all available topics.</span>
      </div>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <div class="nsw-easy-read-ia__node nsw-easy-read-ia__node--child nsw-easy-read-ia__node--category">
            <strong>Category page</strong>
            <span>Lists the detail articles for one topic.</span>
          </div>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <div class="nsw-easy-read-ia__node nsw-easy-read-ia__node--child nsw-easy-read-ia__node--detail">
                <strong>Detail article</strong>
                <span>Provides the Easy Read information.</span>
              </div>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </li>
  </ol>
  <figcaption>Recommended three-level information architecture for larger Easy Read collections.</figcaption>
</figure>
<ul>
  <li>Keep the structure to three levels unless user evidence suggests another category level would help.</li>
  <li>Use breadcrumbs on category and detail pages.</li>
  <li>Link topics directly from the landing page.</li>
  <li>List articles directly from their category page.</li>
  <li>Provide a “Back to all Easy Read topics” link on category pages.</li>
  <li>Use the final About Easy Read callout to link people to more Easy Read articles.</li>
</ul>

