  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <th scope="row">Easy Read content block</th>
      <td>Main information and step-by-step instructions.</td>
      <td><a href="#easy-read-content-block">Easy Read content block section</a></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <th scope="row">Horizontal cards</th>
      <td>Landing and category links to topics or detail pages.</td>
      <td><a href="#easy-read-horizontal-card">Easy Read horizontal card section</a></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <th scope="row">In-page navigation</th>
      <td>Quick movement between sections on longer pages.</td>
      <td><a href="#easy-read-in-page-navigation">Easy Read in-page navigation section</a></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <th scope="row">Utility list</th>
      <td>Downloads and supporting resources.</td>
      <td><a href="#easy-read-utility-list">Easy Read utility list section</a></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <th scope="row">Callout</th>
      <td>Final About Easy Read message and essential supporting context.</td>
      <td><a href="#easy-read-callout">Easy Read callout section</a></td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>Avoid introducing extra components unless user testing shows they improve comprehension. Too many component types on one page can increase cognitive load and make key actions harder to find.</p>

<h4 id="easy-read-content-block">Easy Read content block</h4>
<p>
  The Easy Read content block presents one main idea or action at a time,
  supported by a relevant image. Using this component can help create Easy Read
  content, but does not make a page Easy Read on its own.
</p>
<p>Each content block:</p>
<ul>
  <li>
    Places the image on the left and text on the right, following an established
    Easy Read convention and creating a predictable reading pattern.
  </li>
  <li>
    Uses a white background to keep the content clear and reduce visual
    distraction.
  </li>
  <li>
    Is separated from the next content block by a divider.
  </li>
</ul>

{{#>_docs-example separated=true}}
<div class="nsw-easy-read-content">
  <div class="nsw-easy-read-content__block-stack">
    {{#each model.easy-read-detail.blocks}}
      {{#if-equals @index 0}}
    <div class="nsw-easy-read-content__block-item">
      {{>_easy-read-block this theme="white" headingLevel="h3"}}
    </div>
      {{/if-equals}}
    {{/each}}
    <hr class="nsw-easy-read-content__divider">
    {{#each model.easy-read-detail.blocks}}
      {{#if-equals @index 1}}
    <div class="nsw-easy-read-content__block-item">
      {{>_easy-read-block this theme="white" headingLevel="h3"}}
    </div>
      {{/if-equals}}
    {{/each}}
  </div>
</div>
{{/_docs-example}}

<h4 id="easy-read-horizontal-card">Easy Read horizontal card</h4>
<p>Use a horizontal card with an image, short heading and concise description to link to a topic or detail page. Keep each card on its own row so its image and text remain easy to associate. See the <a href="/components/card/index.html">card component</a> for the full component guidance.</p>

{{#>_docs-example separated=true}}
<div class="nsw-easy-read-content">
  <div class="nsw-grid nsw-grid--spaced">
    <div class="nsw-col nsw-col-12">
      {{#each model.easy-read-landing.categories}}
        {{#if-equals @index 0}}
      {{>_card this horizontal=true highlight=true}}
        {{/if-equals}}
      {{/each}}
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
{{/_docs-example}}

<h4 id="easy-read-in-page-navigation">Easy Read in-page navigation</h4>
<p>Use in-page navigation near the start of a longer article. Write link text that matches the section heading so people can predict where each link goes. See the <a href="/components/in-page-nav/index.html">in-page navigation component</a> for the full component guidance.</p>

{{#>_docs-example separated=true}}
<div class="nsw-easy-read-content">
  <nav class="nsw-in-page-nav" aria-label="On this page">
    <h2 class="nsw-in-page-nav__title">On this page</h2>
    <ul class="nsw-in-page-nav__list">
      {{#each model.easy-read-detail.blocks}}
      <li><a href="#{{id}}">{{title}}</a></li>
      {{/each}}
    </ul>
  </nav>
</div>
{{/_docs-example}}

<h4 id="easy-read-utility-list">Easy Read utility list</h4>
<p>Use the utility list after the main information for actions such as copying, printing, downloading or sharing the page. Give downloads a clear name that describes the format. See the <a href="/components/utility-list/index.html">utility list component</a> for the full component guidance.</p>

{{#>_docs-example separated=true}}
<div class="nsw-easy-read-content">
  {{>_easy-read-downloads-utility model.easy-read-detail.downloads social=model.easy-read-share}}
</div>
{{/_docs-example}}

<h4 id="easy-read-callout">Easy Read callout</h4>
<p>Use the final callout to explain the format and offer useful next steps. Keep it consistent across Easy Read pages and only include links that help people continue their task. See the <a href="/components/callout/index.html">callout component</a> for the full component guidance.</p>

{{#>_docs-example separated=true}}
<div class="nsw-easy-read-content">
  {{>_easy-read-about model.easy-read-detail.about}}
</div>
{{/_docs-example}}

<h3 id="search-results-guidance">Search results for Easy Read content</h3>
<p>When Easy Read articles appear in search results, make the format obvious at a glance and keep category information separate.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Use the <strong>label</strong> to identify the format as <strong>Easy Read</strong>.</li>
  <li>Use <strong>tags</strong> for topic categories, such as health, housing or transport.</li>
  <li>Avoid using category tags as a substitute for the Easy Read format label.</li>
  <li>Apply this pattern consistently across search, related links and index pages that list mixed content types.</li>
</ul>
<p>Use the <a href="/components/list-item/index.html">list item component</a> pattern to present labels and tags consistently in result lists.</p>


<h2 id="typography-and-styles">3. Typography and styles</h2>
<p>The Easy Read type scale is scoped to <code>nsw-easy-read-content</code>. It increases the standard body and heading sizes by 1.25, rounded to the design system's 4px sub-grid. Landing page card titles use the H4 size so they remain distinct from body text. It does not change standard page typography. Values use <code>rem</code> so browser zoom and text resizing continue to work; the pixel values below are equivalents based on the design system's 16px reference size, not a fixed root font size.</p>
<table aria-labelledby="typography-and-styles">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th scope="col">Text</th>
      <th scope="col">Viewport</th>
      <th scope="col">Font size</th>
      <th scope="col">Line height</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><th scope="row">Body</th><td>All</td><td>1.25rem (20px)</td><td>2rem (32px)</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">H1</th><td>Mobile</td><td>2.75rem (44px)</td><td>3.5rem (56px)</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">H1</th><td>Desktop</td><td>3.75rem (60px)</td><td>4.75rem (76px)</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">H2</th><td>Mobile</td><td>2.25rem (36px)</td><td>2.75rem (44px)</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">H2</th><td>Desktop</td><td>2.5rem (40px)</td><td>3.25rem (52px)</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">H3</th><td>Mobile</td><td>1.75rem (28px)</td><td>2.25rem (36px)</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">H3</th><td>Desktop</td><td>2rem (32px)</td><td>2.5rem (40px)</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">H4 and card title, bold</th><td>All</td><td>1.5rem (24px)</td><td>2rem (32px)</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="accessibility-considerations">4. Accessibility considerations</h2>

<h3 id="testing-and-review">Testing and review</h3>
<p>Involve people from the intended audience throughout the work, not only after the page is complete. Include people with intellectual disability and others identified by the research. Pay participants for their expertise and provide enough time, accessible materials and support for meaningful participation.</p>
<p>Use a multi-stage review that covers source facts, Easy Read, accessibility, early words and images, and final usability. Seek review from an appropriate subject matter expert for higher-risk content.</p>
<p>Test the complete experience, not only isolated sentences. Ask people to find information, explain it in their own words and complete the intended task. Record the version tested, what participants found difficult, the changes made and whether significant changes were retested.</p>

<h3 id="accessibility-requirements">Accessibility checks</h3>
<p>Choose checks based on the content, audience, platform and applicable accessibility obligations.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Use semantic headings in a logical hierarchy, such as H2 for top-level article sections and H3 for content blocks nested within them.</li>
  <li>Provide meaningful alternative text for informative images and mark decorative images appropriately.</li>
  <li>Keep the reading and focus order consistent with the visual order.</li>
  <li>Use descriptive links and visible keyboard focus.</li>
  <li>Check keyboard-only use, screen-reader support and text resizing.</li>
  <li>Check reflow at a 320 CSS pixel viewport and at 400% browser zoom. Use the <a href="/docs/content/utilities/reflow.html">Reflow Sass mixin</a> to address targeted layout issues.</li>
  <li>Check light, dark and inverted themes without relying on colour alone.</li>
  <li>Use accessible HTML as the primary format when documents are also available.</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="publication-checklist">Publication checks</h3>
<p>Use this optional checklist to review a page before publication. It is not a substitute for audience feedback.</p>
<p>Check that:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Every factual claim maps to a current authoritative source.</li>
  <li>Essential conditions, exceptions, dates, costs, consequences and ways to get help are present.</li>
  <li>The source owner has approved factual completeness, and the relevant subject matter expert has approved high-risk content.</li>
  <li>Each content block communicates one main concept or action with a matching image purpose.</li>
  <li>Difficult terms are necessary, explained and used consistently.</li>
  <li>Links, phone numbers, service names and download files have been verified.</li>
  <li>Heading structure, alternative text, focus, keyboard use, reflow, zoom and colour contrast have been checked.</li>
  <li>People from the intended audience have tested the complete experience and significant revisions have been retested.</li>
  <li>Any AI disclosure identifies the content created or materially changed using AI.</li>
  <li>The published page records its owner, currency information and next review date.</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="downloadable-formats">Downloadable formats</h3>
<p>Use accessible HTML as the primary version. Offer an accessible PDF or Word version when it helps users. Use EPUB for long-form or collected content where appropriate.</p>
<p>Refer to EPUB Accessibility 1.1 and WCAG 2.2 AA when creating accessible EPUBs. Label files with their format, size and updated date. Check that downloads contain equivalent, current information and that their links and reading order work.</p>
<div class="nsw-callout">
  <div class="nsw-callout__content">
    <p class="nsw-h4">Static download example</p>
    <p><a href="/assets/downloads/how-to-cut-vegetables-safely.epub" download>Download How to Cut Vegetables Safely (EPUB, 367 KB, updated 20 July 2026)</a></p>
  </div>
</div>
<p>This pre-generated example contains the same content and illustrations as the <a href="/templates/content/easy-read/detail.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Easy Read detail article template</a>. Use it to review the package structure and reading experience. Manage creation, accessibility checks and versioning in the CMS or build system rather than the browser.</p>

<h3 id="maintenance">Maintenance and revalidation</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Assign an owner and next review date to the Easy Read page and each downloadable version.</li>
  <li>Review the Easy Read version when its source, service, eligibility rules, contact details or linked resources change.</li>
  <li>Keep HTML and downloadable formats synchronised and remove superseded files.</li>
  <li>Retest with the intended audience after changes that affect meaning, task steps, images, navigation or format.</li>
  <li>Keep a record of the source version, approvals and audience-tested version.</li>
</ul>

